diff --git a/docs/guides/admin-and-loot-trade.en.md b/docs/guides/admin-and-loot-trade.en.md
index be9ad34..bc993a6 100644
--- a/docs/guides/admin-and-loot-trade.en.md
+++ b/docs/guides/admin-and-loot-trade.en.md
@@ -138,6 +138,7 @@ Issued cards display an active **«ISSUED»** badge for the Game Master.
 - **When attempts run out, the Game Master gets "Allow another hack? Yes/No"** over *any* screen, not only inside the Loot/Trade tab. It never appears after a success. **"Yes"** immediately builds a fresh board from the *current* card settings (including edits made while the board was open): new words, hints and brackets, exactly 4 attempts, with the operator sheet preserved. **"No"** leaves the card visible with a **"CLOSED"** badge and disables both "Hack" and "View" for players. Only **"Broadcast again"** in the admin panel, re-issuing the terminal, or re-issuing the location reopens it; an ordinary settings edit does not clear the ban. The same Yes/No buttons and the state ("Available" / "Awaiting decision" / "Closed") are shown in the terminal's live admin panel. Until the GM answers, hacking again is impossible.
 - **A successful hack no longer rebuilds the board.** The **"ACCESS GRANTED"** label and the full hint history stay in the player window, the Game Master receives a **"Terminal hacked"** notice over any screen with the operator name, and the live card panel shows a **"Hacked"** state. Neither reopening the window, nor a second browser tab, nor a reconnect erases the win: only the GM **"Broadcast again"** starts a new run. When attempts do run out, discovered hints such as `5/7` remain on the failure screen.
 - **Terminal access is one-shot per broadcast.** The first **"Hack"** press is tied to a broadcast id issued by the server: closing the window no longer restores the button, and after a success, a failure or a "No" answer both card buttons are disabled with a state badge. Only **"Broadcast again"**, re-issuing the location, or the GM answering **"Yes"** reopens it. The hacked notice reaches the GM in any browser tab, even when the interface mode switch is shared: ownership comes from the room member list. **"Leave location"** sits next to **"Minimize"** — that player disconnects while the scene stays open for everyone else; only the GM can end it for all.
+- **Handover.** Snapshots sync from the GM Google Drive in both directions, so the save arrives on another device. Both the GM and players can export storage to a file and import it back: when the GM changes, the new GM fills storage on the new server in one action. Room state lives only in server memory and disappears on disconnect — the file and Drive are the recovery path. See `docs/patch-509-install.md`.
 - **"Shared storage"** is available from the player inventory: a full-width green bar below "Add loot" / "Buy items". Items are shared across the room: anyone can deposit or withdraw, and an absent player picks up what was left later. Storage belongs to the GM: its snapshot is saved in the GM local database and Google Drive, while the server keeps only the current in-memory room state. A connecting player receives the current snapshot; without the GM online storage is read-only. The GM may deposit but cannot take back what players already own; the name is not customizable. See `docs/key-items-and-gates-design.md` and `docs/patch-507-install.md`.
 - The dictionary holds 12,308 real English words in six per-length files, so consecutive terminals no longer reuse the same candidates. At difficulty 1–5 the board has **three groups** of words sharing a combination (ending, prefix or suffix), each with **two variants** of that combination — e.g. `TION`/`SION`, `NESS`/`LESS`, `THE`/`THO`. A hint first reveals the right group, then the right sequence inside it; extra words lower blind-guess odds. Hints can reach `L−1` matching letters out of `L`: a high hint is fine because it requires deduction, not brute force. The password is revealed by neither the "most similar to everything" nor the "least similar" heuristic. Difficulty 0 keeps its special anagram families and outlier password.
 - Three stats act separately. **INT 4–10** sets board size `N = max(4, 15 + 2·difficulty − INT)`: at difficulty 5 that is "25 − INT", i.e. 21 words at INT 4 and 15 at INT 10. **Science 0–6** sets the number of brackets at every difficulty — `0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4`. Brackets come in two kinds: some remove one wrong word, others restore one attempt; a single bracket does exactly one effect. At Science 6 there are four brackets: two for words and two for attempts. **Luck 4–10** gives a 5–20% chance of a full attempt restore, but such a restore can appear **at most once per board**. **Luck points** are never spent while hacking — writing them off before or after and declaring the terminal solved is a GM table decision.
